<TD>
<PRE>
</PRE>
<FONT SIZE=4>Consider guitarist/singer <B>Paul Savoy</B> is holed up for an Arctic winter in his Oslo basement, all alone with only his
songwriter/film maker wife Lauren and 800 square feet  of recording equipment to keep him warm.  What else can a world-class performer do but make the album of his life.  That's the engaging scenario behind <B>MARY IS COMING </B>the gallant debut Warner
Bros. album from <B>SAVOY.</B>
<BR><BR>
Known planetwide as a founding member of <FONT SIZE=5><B>a-ha</B><FONT SIZE=4>, Paul had no iron-clad objectives when he and Lauren set out to write and record MARY IS COMING other than to have a good time making music.   In fact, the album emerged incrementally in all its organic splendor.  "In the past,  a lot of time would be spent going through sound after sound," Paul says.  " It always became a chore. Here the idea was to choose a few favorite sounds and go from there."   An embarrassment of shortages?  Hardly. Though undeniably fat-free, Savoy's music revels in taut muscularity,  highlighted by Paul's virtouso guitar playing and plaitive vocals.
<BR><BR>
Written, produced and engineered completely by Paul and Lauren, <B>MARY IS COMING</B> is indeed a marked departure from Paul's previous recorded work.  Paul himself played guitar and bass, with drummer Frode Unneland an Lauren's guitar and background providing the only accompaniment.
  </TR></TABLE>
<FONT SIZE=4><P>
The albums rough-hewn sound was deliberate. " I tend to write very melodically," notes Paul,
"and so in the arrangements I try to avoid too much sugar on top.  The melodic elements come out much more when they have something to hit against.  If we didn't get it in two takes,  we moved on.  I like a trashy feel."
<BR><BR>
<TABLE CELLSPACING=0 CELLPADDING=0>
<TR>
<TD>
<FONT SIZE=4>
Trashy, you say?  From the opening notes of the corrosive lead track <B>"Daylight's Wasting"</B>, MARY IS COMING offers its own coarse version of salvation.  Songs like the smoldering<B>"Foolish," "Tears From A Stone," </B>the harmonically rich <B>"We Will Never Forget,"</B>
and the fuzz-filtered salute to self -sacrifice <B>"Get Up Now,"</B> exemplify Savoy's serrated guitar-centered approach.
On the other hand, the fragile ballad <B>"Velvet," "1/2 Hours Worth," </B>and the simple elegy" Mary is coming," all display Savoy's ample Soul ... of the Rubber variety.
<BR><BR>
<TD><CENTER><IMG SRC="IMAGE/savwave.jpg">
</TR></TABLE>
<FONT SIZE=4>
An accomplished film maker, lyricist <B>Lauren Savoy</B> is a born story teller.  Her economy of style dovetails with her husband's songwriting tactics.  " We both love melodies," says Lauren, " and we're determined not to get too fuzzy.  We wanted to keep things simple. It ended up unlike anything Paul's done before.  I think this album must have been brewing inside him for a long time.
<BR><BR>
Not that he had had much time before to record it.  Along with <B>Mags Furuholmen</B> and <B>Morten Harket</B> Paul was, and is, part of one
of the world's, and certainly Norway's,  biggest bands.  <B> With well over twenty million album sold, eight MTV awards, a Grammy nomination,  14 #1 hits worldwide, and a #1 US single  ("Take On Me" in 1985), a-ha enjoyed an extravagant run throughout the world.  </B>After six best-selling albums, and nearly non-stop touring, the band decided to take a much-needed break in 1994,
giving members a chance to catch their breath an recharge their batteries.
<BR><BR>
<TABLE CELLSPACING=0 CELLPADDING=0>
<TR>
<TD><CENTER><IMG SRC="IMAGE/lauren.jpg" align=middle>
<br></CENTER>
<TD>
<FONT SIZE=4>
Meanwhile, <B>Lauren Savoy</B> had been busy with a career of her own.  The Boston-born Lauren met Paul while attending film school in London.  Paul was in the process of forming a-ha, so Lauren went along with him on the band's decade-long magic carpet ride, but only intermittently.  An accomplished film maker, Lauren has directed scores of music videos in Europe,  and created several short films as well.  Thanks to their thoroughly modern marriage, each helped the other's career when possible.
With her acute story telling skills, Lauren often wrote lyrics for Paul, much as the Norwegian would offer suggestions to her in the film editing room.
<BR><BR>
</TR></TABLE>
<FONT SIZE=4>
Making <B>MARY IS COMING</B> was nevertheless a watershed event for the couple.  " We had to learn how to use all this equipment,"
recalls Lauren," But even though sometimes sound would leak, the rawness ended up being very effective."
After completing the album, the pair on their own created a music video for the song <B>"Velvet"</B>.  With master and video in hand, the two knocked on Warner Bros. door, which flung open quickly.  Now, Savoy is a working band, with Paul, Lauren, and Frode joined
by American bassist <B> Greg Calvert</B>.  The quartet is tuning up for a string of U.S. showcase dates to come in the winter and spring.
<BR><BR>
So how does Paul feel about staring over after ten years in the spotlight?  Not bad, apparently. " I love starting from scratch,"
he says, " To begin fresh and build something from a natural staring point is great.  We are definitely ready to roll."
With the release of Savoy's sensational debut album,  Paul's fiery sentiment should come as no surprise.  The album may have begun life as basement tapes, but now <B> Savoy's MARY IS COMING </B>has unquestionably risen to the rooftops.
<BR><BR>
